$DIR/main.p:30:6: s holds a view of b (b.b, line 27), which was moved by the call b.Consume() (Consume has a value receiver) at line 29, so s can no longer be used here
    s looks at memory that b owns, without owning it: the view was made by b.b
    at line 27. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be used only
    while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At line 29 b was
    moved by the call b.Consume() (Consume has a value receiver), so the view
    may look at memory that was released, or at a value that now belongs to
    something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use s before line 29, or move b only after the last use of s
    Or move a copy at line 29, so that b keeps its value:
        b.Clone()
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

